// @title   TropeLang Concept Library — Virtues and Vices
// @version 1.3
// @note    Moral qualities that narrative traditions invoke across all cultures.
//          These are ideals and their opposites — not behavioral facts but
//          evaluative orientations. They appear as targets of directed states
//          ([~Devoted_To(target=courage)]) and as tags on characters
//          ([+Courageous]).
//
// Import: import "trl/concepts/virtues.trl"


// =============================================================================
// VIRTUES — qualities held as good, worth cultivating or embodying
// =============================================================================

// The capacity to act despite fear or danger; action in the presence of risk.
// Courage is not the absence of fear but the willingness to proceed despite it.
concept courage        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The integration of self — acting consistently with one's stated values.
// Integrity is tested precisely when deviation would be advantageous.
concept integrity      [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract] [+Fragile]

// The quality of acting rightly toward others; giving each their due.
// Justice as virtue is the disposition; justice as concept is the system.
concept justice_virtue [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The ability to understand deeply and judge well across circumstances.
// Wisdom is experiential — it cannot be transmitted without being earned.
concept wisdom         [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The disposition to feel and respond to the suffering of others.
// Compassion bridges self and other; it motivates without erasing difference.
concept compassion     [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The quality of giving more than required; sharing resources or care beyond obligation.
// Generosity transforms relationships from transactional to relational.
concept generosity     [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The accurate assessment of one's own place and limitations.
// Humility is not self-deprecation; it is clear-eyed non-inflation of self.
concept humility       [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract] [+Fragile]

// The condition of reliable, principled faithfulness to a person, cause, or covenant.
// Loyalty is bidirectional in expectation but unilateral in practice.
concept loyalty        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The capacity to bear difficulty without breaking; persistence through adversity.
// Endurance is not passivity — it is active maintenance of self under pressure.
concept endurance      [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The quality of treating others fairly and without prejudice.
// Fairness operates at the procedural level; justice operates at the outcome level.
concept fairness       [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// VICES — qualities held as harmful, corrupting, or destructive
// =============================================================================

// The excessive desire for more than one's due — material or immaterial.
// Greed distorts the relation between self and world; it is never satisfied.
concept greed          [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The excessive regard for one's own importance; inflation of self beyond reality.
// Pride in this sense differs from earned confidence; it refuses correction.
concept pride_excess   [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The desire for what another has, combined with resentment of their possession.
// Envy is relational — it cannot exist without comparison to another.
concept envy           [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract] [#Painful]

// The state of avoiding necessary effort or action; inertia in the face of obligation.
// Sloth may be physical, moral, or intellectual.
concept sloth          [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The disproportionate response to injury or insult; rage that exceeds its cause.
// Wrath is force beyond what the situation warrants; it harms the holder as well as the target.
concept wrath          [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// Dishonesty maintained over time; the sustained misrepresentation of truth.
// Deceit erodes the trust that makes all other bonds possible.
concept deceit         [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The systematic disregard for the rights, pain, or dignity of others.
// Cruelty may be deliberate or structural; both cause harm.
concept cruelty        [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The state of having been corrupted by power, gain, or temptation.
// Corruption is a process as much as a state — it occurs gradually.
concept corruption     [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]

// The failure to act against wrong when action was possible.
// Complicity may be silent, indirect, or rationalized; it enables harm.
concept complicity     [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]
